  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nCURRENT OVERNIGHT - TODAY:\n\nA dry cold front is currently moving southeast across northeast\nMinnesota and the Twin Ports and will move through northwest \nWisconsin through early this morning. This will switch winds to \nwesterly and northwesterly and bring increasing clouds behind \nthe front. Expect the immediate frontal passage to remain dry \ngiven dry low to middle portions of the atmosphere. Lows bottom \nout in the mid 30s to around 40 degrees, though slightly warmer \nnear Lake Superior.\n\nBreezy northwest winds with gusts of 20 to 30 mph develop for \ntoday in many locations before weakening rapidly toward sunset.\nSome wind gusts of 30 to locally 40 mph are forecast for the\nNorth Shore from early this morning through midday, particularly\nfor the higher terrain ridge. There is some signal in the models\nfor these winds to reach lower elevations near Lake Superior as\nkatabatic winds, but this is not certain and will bear watching\nearly this morning. If that were to occur, some brief gale-force\ngusts may push into the nearshore waters for areas around Silver\nBay to Grand Portage. Despite the dry frontal passage, cyclonic\nflow and some shortwave energy passing though the Borderlands\ninto portions of the Arrowhead could produce brief sprinkles \nand perhaps a few very light showers. Very little to no \nprecipitation accumulation is expected from this activity. High \ntemperatures will be about 5-7 F on warm side of normal today \nranging from the mid 40s to low 50s, warmest in east- central \nMinnesota to northwest Wisconsin.\n\nMONDAY - TUESDAY:\n\nMonday and Tuesday will be seasonably warm with highs in the \nupper 40s to mid 50s on Monday and mid 40s to mid 50s on\nTuesday. This period looks largely dry. There will be a cold \nfrontal passage on Monday and with the main energy with the\nshortwave trough mainly post-frontal. CAMs are suggesting some\nlow-precipitation potential with the front late Monday morning\ninto afternoon for northern Minnesota and the Arrowhead, but a\nwedge of dry air in the 700 mb to surface layer favors the\nfrontal passage being dry without precipitation reaching the\nground. The chance for any measurable precipitation (0.01\" or\ngreater) is less than 20%. \n\nREST OF THIS WEEK:\n\nNo significant changes from the previous forecast. A lobe of\nmid-level vorticity moves north to south across the Northland\nTuesday night through Thursday, bringing colder 850 mb\ntemperatures (-5 to -10 degC) with it that will promote light\nlake-effect precipitation along the South Shore due to a\nrelatively warm Lake Superior water surface of 7 to 10 degC \nabove zero. While this precipitation will be largely rain\ndaytime Wednesday, precipitation transitions to mainly snow\nWednesday evening into daytime Thursday. Synoptic moisture with\nthis system is limited, so snowfall accumulations look light at\nthis time for the South Shore snowbelts. Precipitation is\nforecast to come to an end by late Thursday with a dry Friday as\ndrier air and high pressure move into the Upper Midwest.\n\nNear-normal temperatures set up for the middle to later portion\nof the work week with high temperatures in the upper 30s to mid\n40s before warming above normal again next weekend as southerly\nwinds return at the surface and ridging aloft approaches from\nthe west.",
